"Intro astronomy; upper level astrophysics (stellar remnants, plasma physics)",
"A white dwarf has no fuel. It shines by leaking heat stored in its ions; the degenerate electrons store almost none.",
"The thin envelope is the insulation and sets L ∝ M T_c^{#}. With U = ({#}/{#})NkT_c this gives Mestel's law, t ≈ ({#}/{#})U/L and L ∝ t^(−{#}/{#}): fast fading while hot, ever slower later.",
"The ions freeze where the coupling Γ = (Ze)²/({#}πε₀ a kT) passes about {#}. Γ grows as ρ^({#}/{#})/T, so freezing starts at the dense center, and heavier (denser) white dwarfs freeze first, while still bright.",
"Freezing releases latent heat ({#} kT per ion), so crystallization slows the fading.",
"This page is a textbook model. Its envelope makes a {#} M☉ white dwarf start to crystallize later and fainter (T_eff about {#} K) than detailed models (about {#} K), so read its trends, not its exact numbers.",
"Students need L = {#}πR²σT_eff⁴ and ({#}/{#})kT per particle. Three panels: luminosity (or <strong>Core T</strong>) against cooling age, a slice through the star with its Γ profile, and a schematic Gaia HR diagram. Drag the age line, the dot on the HR diagram, or the handle on the star's surface (mass); the <strong>Mass M</strong> and <strong>Cooling age t</strong> sliders do the same. Checkboxes under <strong>Crystallization</strong> turn freezing and latent heat on and off; <strong>Liquid ions</strong> switches between <strong>Ideal gas</strong> and <strong>Coulomb liquid</strong>. Turn on <strong>Projector</strong> for the room.",
"<strong>Hot young white dwarf.</strong> A {#} M☉ carbon/oxygen dwarf {#} years old: about {#} L☉, T_eff ≈ {#} K. Let it play and watch the dot slide down its HR track. Ask the class where its light comes from.",
"<strong>Mestel cooling law.</strong> Ideal gas ions, no freezing. At {#} Myr the curve has joined the dashed L ∝ t^(−{#}/{#}) line (slope −{#}). Work Q2 with the rail's numbers, then switch to <strong>Coulomb liquid</strong>: the curve moves right, because the liquid stores more heat per ion.",
"<strong>Crystallization begins.</strong> A {#} M☉ dwarf at {#} Gyr: {#}% of its mass is solid, but the front is only at {#} R. The Γ panel shows Γ = {#} at the center. Point at the dotted curve (no latent heat) and the <strong>Delay from latent heat</strong> readout (+{#} Myr); ask Q5.",
"<strong>Heavier dwarfs crystallize first.</strong> Ask Q1 before you press it. The {#} M☉ onset is at {#} Gyr and M_G ≈ {#}, where Gaia found the Q branch pile up. Drag the mass handle and watch <strong>Crystallization begins</strong> and <strong>Center freezes at</strong> move (Q4).",
"<strong>Helium vs carbon/oxygen.</strong> {#} times as many ions in the same mass: brighter at the same age, and Γ is about {#} times smaller, so it does not freeze within the age of the universe (Q6).",
"<strong>Cosmic clock.</strong> A simulated disk {#} Gyr old. Drag the gold line and the faint end of the counts (<strong>Faintest {#}%</strong>) moves with it. Finish with Q7: this {#} M☉ dwarf starts freezing at T_eff {#} K (<strong>Crystallization begins</strong>), where detailed models give about {#} K.",
"<strong>Q1.</strong> Predict: two carbon/oxygen white dwarfs form at the same time, one of {#} M☉ and one of {#} M☉. Which starts to crystallize first, and is it brighter or fainter than the other at that moment? Then check with <strong>Heavier dwarfs crystallize first</strong>.",
"<strong>Q2.</strong> In the <strong>Mestel cooling law</strong> preset at a cooling age of {#} Myr, the rail shows {#} × {#}⁵⁵ ions, a core temperature T_c = {#} × {#}⁷ K and L = {#} × {#}⁻³ L☉ (L☉ = {#} × {#}²⁶ W). Find the stored heat U = ({#}/{#})NkT_c and Mestel's age t = ({#}/{#})U/L, and compare with the age. (k = {#} × {#}⁻²³ J/K, {#} yr = {#} × {#}⁷ s.)",
"<strong>Q3.</strong> In Mestel's model L ∝ T_c^{#} and U ∝ T_c. Show that late in life L ∝ t^(−{#}/{#}). If a white dwarf takes {#} Gyr to fade to some luminosity, how old is it when ten times fainter? Check the slope readout.",
